Cost-based trigger for scroll degradation (WEBKIT_LOWRES_COST_TRIGGER, default OFF).

The existing ladder decides whether to degrade from scroll SPEED. Speed is a
geometry term: it says how fast tiles are being exposed, not what a tile costs to
rasterize -- and cost is what decides whether degrading helps or hurts. Measured
on build 618/620 with the identical WEBKIT_LOWRES_TILE_SCALE=0.3 knob:

  raster-bound page (282 ns/px, 13.0 ms/tile)  p95 76ms -> 44ms  (-42%)
  cheap page        (11.9 ns/px,  0.8 ms/tile) p95 27ms -> 29ms  (+7%),
                                               jank 1.3% -> 3.4%

Raster cost separates those pages by 23.7x while their scroll velocities are
indistinguishable, so no velocity threshold can tell them apart. Re-calibration
was tested and rejected: raising the thresholds halved engagement on the cheap
page with ZERO change to its penalty, while giving up a quarter of the
raster-bound page's tail win.

This trigger asks the question that determines the sign of the outcome -- will
the tiles this pass must paint fit in a frame?

  predictedMs = tileRasterNsPerPixel * pixelsToPaint      (EWMA from the
                                                           instrumentation patch)
  overshoot   = predictedMs / WEBKIT_LOWRES_COST_BUDGET_MS

engaging only when overshoot > 1. The scale is DERIVED, not fixed: raster cost is
~linear in painted pixels and scale s paints s^2 of them, so 1/sqrt(overshoot) is
the scale that just fits the budget. Degradation becomes proportional to overload
instead of a cliff. On the measured numbers a 3-tile exposure is 0.15x budget on
the cheap page (never degrades, at any speed) and 2.35x on the raster-bound one
(scale 0.65 -- fits the budget and is far less visible than the current 0.3x).

Asymmetric hysteresis: engage on one over-budget pass, disengage only after 4
consecutive passes below 70% of budget. Motivated by measurement -- at 43-46%
armed the heavy page's jank returned to baseline while raster CPU stayed
elevated, because every engage/disengage cycle costs a whole-tile full-res
sharpen repaint. A trigger that hovers near its threshold is worse than one that
stays either side of it.

Decision runs in updateIfNeeded (first point where the pass's actual work is
known) rather than setVisibleRect, and velocity arming is disabled while the cost
trigger owns the tier so the two cannot fight. Unknown cost (no EWMA sample yet)
means DO NOT degrade: guessing cheap costs one soft frame, guessing expensive
blurs a page that never needed it.

Knobs: WEBKIT_LOWRES_COST_TRIGGER=1 (default off), _BUDGET_MS (11),
_SCALE_FLOOR (0.3), _CHECKERBOARD_X (11), _DISENGAGE_PASSES (4).
WEBKIT_SCROLLTIER_LOG=1 traces every decision as [costtier].

Requires webkit-tile-raster-cost-instrumentation.patch. Must apply after it and
after webkit-lowres-tiles-during-scroll-env.patch / -cpu-path.patch.

UPDATE (device testing on franceinfo.fr): the first version degraded STATIONARY
pages. Three fixes:

  * scroll gate -- degrade only within WEBKIT_LOWRES_COST_MOTION_MS (120) of real
    scroll movement. The speed ladder got this implicitly by living inside the
    dy != 0 branch of setVisibleRect; evaluating cost on every layer flush lost
    it, so the trigger engaged during load and on idle repaints. Observed: 99
    layer proxies pinned at the 0.3 floor on an idle page.
  * it also exposed a LATENT bug in the existing low-res path -- lowResBufferSize
    rounds up (ceil), so a 256px tile at 0.3 paints 76.8 of 77 texels and the
    unpainted fraction upscales into a seam at every tile edge. Transient while
    degradation is brief; held at rest it becomes a permanent dark GRID over the
    page. The gate makes it transient again; the rounding itself still wants a
    separate fix.
  * engage margin WEBKIT_LOWRES_COST_ENGAGE_X (2.0) -- never engage above scale
    ~0.71. Engaging costs a full-res repaint of every degraded tile, so a
    marginal engagement loses; measured, a 0.90 scale still paid the whole
    +1.9pp jank penalty. Also addresses "low res shows too early".
  * disengage immediately when motion stops, bypassing the hysteresis, so tiles
    sharpen at rest instead of staying soft for several more flushes.

UPDATE 2 (franceinfo, build 623): the scroll gate never fired on a real page --
964 of 964 samples reported scrolling=0 while the page was being flung, so the
trigger was inert. Cause: the motion timestamp was PER-PROXY. A real page is many
layers (~99 here) and each proxy only runs when its own layer changes, so any one
layer's last-motion timestamp is routinely older than the window. This is the same
per-layer sampling sparsity that makes the speed ladder's `dt < 0.2` gate discard
536 of 536 samples and never arm on a real page at all.

Whether the user is scrolling is a property of the PAGE, not of a layer: a fixed
header never moves and a parallax layer moves at its own rate, yet the page is
scrolling in both cases. Motion is now recorded process-wide (relaxed atomic),
written by whichever layer observes movement and read by all of them, which is
both correct and immune to per-layer sparsity. Window widened to 250 ms to span
the gaps between flushes on a heavy page.

UPDATE 3 (build 625): **default flipped ON**, and the checkerboard rung defaulted
OFF. Device-validated by the user on real browsing after the page-global motion
fix. On real reddit scrolling the legacy speed ladder degraded 94% of samples --
73% of them CHECKERBOARD (blank bands) at an effective 267 CSS px/s -- while this
uses no checkerboard at all and sits at a derived low-res ~0.40-0.42.

The binary defaults and deploy/runtime-common.sh now agree deliberately: having
them disagree is the trap recorded as C4, where the shipped thresholds were 3-4x
lower than the source implied and every conclusion drawn from reading the C++ was
wrong.

